# What forest-shell tells shell-switch about itself (#57). Data only — sourced,
# never executed, by tools/register-shell-switch.sh and tools/binds-harness.sh.
#
# These are the values that end up in the `SHELL_DB` associative array in
# ~/.config/shell-switch/lib/shell-manager.sh. That file is an uncommitted local
# modification to a checkout of someone else's GitLab repo — the ghibli entry
# already in it is the precedent — so this file is the only copy of them that
# survives a `git checkout` over there.
#
# All five properties except `id` are load-bearing: omitting one makes
# shell-switch's `get_shell_info` log an ERROR and return 1, which under the
# caller's `set -euo pipefail` aborts the switch.

# The caller sets this to the repo root before sourcing — every command below is
# an absolute path into the checkout, and this file has no way to find it.
: "${FOREST_REPO:?set FOREST_REPO to the repo root before sourcing registration.env}"

FOREST_ID="forest"
FOREST_NAME="Forest Shell"

# ## Why the direct path, and not a config-dir symlink
#
# Quickshell can reach a config either way: `-c <name>` resolves
# $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/quickshell/<name>/shell.qml, and `-p <path>` takes the file
# directly. The shell-switch research (§3 step 0, A1) recommended the symlink,
# on the grounds that it is shorter and matches how ghibli is installed.
#
# That recommendation was overruled before this ticket existed. The assembly
# refinements on #13 close A1 explicitly — "direct-path launch (`qs -p
# <repo>/shell.qml`, `qs-upstream` wrapper until the swap phase, no config-dir
# symlink)" — and note #12 had already settled it. #57 cites that comment as a
# source and does not reopen it, so the direct path is what is registered here.
# The research file is older than the decision; where they disagree, the
# decision wins.

# launch_cmd is run as `eval "$launch_cmd" &>/dev/null &`. It must stay a
# foreground, non-daemonising command: shell-switch backgrounds it itself and
# then looks for it with `pgrep -f`, so an added `&`, `disown` or `--daemonize`
# either changes the cmdline out from under process_pattern or detaches the
# process it is waiting to see.
FOREST_LAUNCH_CMD="qs -p $FOREST_REPO/shell.qml"

# launcher_cmd is emitted verbatim into `bind = SUPER, Space, exec, <it>`.
#
# Both commands are interpolated with `sed -e "s|{{VAR}}|${value}|g"`, so
# neither may contain `|` — it ends the sed expression — or `&`, which means
# "the whole match" in a sed replacement. An absolute path is fine: `/` is not
# the delimiter, and the path has no spaces, which also keeps the niri
# `{{LAUNCH_CMD_ARGS}}` form (naive whitespace splitting) correct. That
# constraint is the entire reason SUPER+Space is the one bind in this shell with
# no `|| fallback`, and tools/binds-harness.sh checks the two facts against each
# other.
FOREST_LAUNCHER_CMD="qs -p $FOREST_REPO/shell.qml ipc call launcher toggle"

# Fed to `pgrep -f` and `pkill -f` as an unanchored substring of the full
# command line. Two hazards, both real: it must not match another registered
# shell — `detect_running_shell` takes the first hit in `get_all_shells` order
# and `stop_shell` would kill the wrong process — and it must not be a prefix of
# something longer that is also running. The existing patterns are
# `qs.*noctalia-shell`, `dms run` and `qs -c ghibli`; none of them contains this
# path and this contains none of them.
#
# Naming shell.qml rather than the directory is deliberate: the harnesses launch
# other entry points out of the same checkout (`capture-harness.qml`,
# `lock-harness.qml`), and a directory-shaped pattern would match those too —
# so a switch away from forest-shell would kill a running capture.
FOREST_PROCESS_PATTERN="qs -p $FOREST_REPO/shell.qml"

# Probed with `pacman -Q`, and only ever by install.sh, which nothing here runs.
# A config directory has no package of its own, so the convention is to name the
# runtime instead: ghibli declares `noctalia-qs` for this reason. Post-#57 the
# runtime is the real `quickshell` package, so unlike ghibli's entry this one
# happens to be true rather than a convenient fiction.
FOREST_PACKAGES="quickshell"
